{
  "recap": "Models can be made smaller and more efficient using techniques like distillation and compression. Distillation can transfer knowledge across different data types (modalities) or from large ensembles into a single small model. Furthermore, matching internal representations, rather than just final outputs, can lead to cleaner and more effective learning.",
  "review": [
    {
      "key_point": "S10.K1",
      "question": "How can distillation be used to bridge the gap when labeled data is available for one type of input but not another?",
      "answer": "A teacher model trained on one modality (like RGB) can transfer its knowledge to a student model that only processes a different modality (like depth)."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S10.K2",
      "question": "Why might one choose to distill the results of an ensemble rather than simply running all the large models simultaneously?",
      "answer": "An ensemble of models is often highly predictive, but distilling their collective output allows for a much more efficient, single model that retains much of that predictive power."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S10.K3",
      "question": "What is the difference between standard knowledge distillation and representation distillation?",
      "answer": "Standard distillation matches the final output scores (logits), while representation distillation focuses on making the internal embeddings or representations of the student model similar to the teacher's."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S10.K4",
      "question": "How do the goals and methods of model distillation and model compression differ?",
      "answer": "Distillation uses training (SGD) to teach a small model the characteristics of a large one, whereas compression techniques like pruning or quantization reduce the computational cost of an existing large model."
