    {
      "key_point": "S6.K1",
      "question": "What are the two primary operations that occur during a single layer application of a Graph Net?",
      "answer": "The process involves neighbors sending messages to each other, followed by an aggregation step that uses those messages to update the receiving node's vector."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S6.K2",
      "question": "Why is it crucial that the aggregation function be permutation-invariant when processing a graph?",
      "answer": "Permutation invariance ensures that the resulting message is independent of the order in which the neighbors are processed or indexed."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S6.K3",
      "question": "Mathematically, what kind of function is the aggregate function, and what does it map from and to?",
      "answer": "It is a multiset function that takes a set of input items (the neighbor node vectors) and produces a single output vector."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S6.K4",
      "question": "Name two common examples of aggregation functions and describe their relationship to operations found in ConvNets.",
      "answer": "Examples include summation and max/min. Max/min functions are analogous to pooling operations in convolutional networks."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S6.K5",
      "question": "How does running the Graph Net algorithm for multiple rounds (layers) affect the amount of information a node can gather from its surroundings?",
      "answer": "Each application of the two-step process increases the node's receptive field by one, allowing it to gather information from nodes further away in subsequent rounds."
    }
  ],
