{
  "recap": "The primary challenge in generative modeling is that the true distribution of latent variables given the data, $p(Z|X)$, is often unknown and complicated. To address this, an encoder function, $q$, is trained to map the input data $X$ to an approximate distribution over the latent space $Z$. This encoder minimizes the KL divergence between its approximation and the true distribution, which is then used in the overall generative model objective to maximize data likelihood via importance sampling.",
  "review": [
    {
      "key_point": "S11.K1",
      "question": "In the context of generative modeling, why is the distribution of latent variables given the input data, $p(Z|X)$, often difficult to determine directly?",
      "answer": "It is often a complicated distribution that cannot be easily modeled or calculated analytically, requiring a new method to approximate it."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S11.K2",
      "question": "What is the primary function of the encoder model, $q$, in a Variational Autoencoder (VAE)?",
      "answer": "The encoder takes the input data $X$ and outputs a density over the latent space $Z$, effectively mapping the data to an inferred distribution."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S11.K3",
      "question": "What specific divergence measure is used to train the encoder, $q$, to make its predicted distribution match the true distribution $p(Z|X)$?",
      "answer": "The KL divergence is used to quantify and minimize the difference between the true distribution and the distribution approximated by the encoder."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S11.K4",
      "question": "How does the overall generative model objective, $J_p$, utilize the learned encoder distribution $q$ to estimate the likelihood of the data?",
      "answer": "It uses importance sampling, where the learned distribution $q$ acts as the importance sampling distribution to estimate the integral defining the data likelihood."
    }
  ],
  "explanations": [

  ]
}

{
  "recap": "Naive Monte Carlo sampling is inefficient because most random samples of the latent variable $z$ will place nearly zero probability on the target data point $x$. Importance sampling addresses this by changing the sampling distribution to focus only on the $z$'s that contribute highly to the likelihood. The ideal distribution to sample from is the conditional density $p(z|x)$.",
  "review": [
    {
      "key_point": "S10.K1",
      "question": "Why is using naive Monte Carlo sampling to estimate the likelihood of a data point $x$ often inefficient?",
      "answer": "Most randomly sampled latent variables ($z$) will result in mixture components that place nearly zero probability on the target data point $x$, meaning most samples contribute nothing to the expectation."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S10.K2",
      "question": "How does importance sampling mathematically transform an expectation calculation to allow for a more efficient Monte Carlo approximation?",
      "answer": "It involves multiplying the original expectation by a new density $q(z)$ and then sampling from $q(z)$ instead of the original density $p(z)$."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S10.K3",
      "question": "What is the fundamental conceptual goal of importance sampling when estimating a likelihood?",
      "answer": "The goal is to sample from a distribution that concentrates its density on the specific points ($z$'s) that are most likely to contribute high probability to the target data point $x$."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S10.K4",
      "question": "Theoretically, what distribution provides the minimum variance when performing the expectation estimation?",
      "answer": "The optimal distribution is the conditional density $p(z|x)$, as sampling from this distribution ensures that the samples are concentrated exactly where they matter for the data point $x$."
    }
  ],
  "explanations": [

  ]
}

