{
  "recap": "Generalization theory often starts with Occam's razor, which suggests that the simplest model that fits the data is the most likely to generalize well. It is critical that the model actually fits the data, otherwise the principle is meaningless. While a rigorous version suggests the shortest program generating the data is the best model, finding this program is computationally intractable.",
