    },
    {
      "key_point": "S7.K2",
      "question": "Why is it crucial to include the condition 'fits the data' when applying Occam's razor to model selection?",
      "answer": "Without this condition, the principle leads to absurd conclusions, such as preferring a simple model that fails to explain the observed data."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S7.K3",
      "question": "What specific concept, derived from algorithmic information theory, provides a mathematically rigorous version of Occam's razor?",
      "answer": "This rigorous version suggests that the model corresponding to the shortest program that perfectly generates the data will generalize the best."
    },
    {
      "key_point": "S7.K4",
      "question": "Why is the mathematically rigorous approach (finding the shortest program) not practically useful in deep learning?",
